{"title":"Forming regular and irregular plurals","key_idea":"Most nouns add -s or -es to make plural, but some nouns change in special ways.","method":["Look at how the word ends to decide which rule to follow","For words ending in -x, -ch, -sh, or -s, add -es to the singular form","For words ending in a consonant and then -y, change the y to i and add -es","For some words, the spelling changes completely inside the word instead of just adding a sound at the end"],"worked_example":{"problem":"Write the plural of: box, dish, berry","steps":["Box ends in -x, so add -es: boxes","Dish ends in -sh, so add -es: dishes","Berry ends in consonant plus -y, so change y to i and add -es: berries"],"answer":"boxes, dishes, berries"},"key_terms":[{"term":"singular","meaning":"one person, animal, or thing"},{"term":"plural","meaning":"more than one person, animal, or thing"},{"term":"irregular plural","meaning":"a plural that does not follow the normal adding-s rule"}],"common_mistakes":["Adding only -s to box (boxs instead of boxes)","Keeping the y in berry instead of changing it to berries"],"remember":"Box → boxes, child → children, leaf → leaves: remember these three because they do not all follow the same pattern.","quick_check":[{"q":"Write the plural of glass.","a":"glasses (ends in -s, so add -es)"},{"q":"Write the plural of person.","a":"people (irregular; it changes completely)"}],"tell_your_child":"Some words just add -s, but box and leaf are tricky—box gets -es, and leaf changes its middle letters to leaves. Child is even more special: it becomes children, not childs."}
